Part 2: Event-Driven Architecture (EDA) & Asynchronous Communication
3. Asynchronous Communication:
Using @Async annotations and ThreadPoolTaskExecutor to execute methods in the background.
This is utilized within services to communicate with other services asynchronously after state updates.
Part 3: Implementing Saga Pattern
4. Saga Pattern:
a. TransactionService:
Initiate Transaction: Commits a pending transaction and emits an event for AccountService.
Handle Failures: Listens for failure events and compensates the transaction.
b. AccountService:
Update Account: Updates account balances and emits success/failure events.
Handle Failures: Listens for failure events to revert any performed changes.
c. NotificationService:
Send Notifications: Listens for success/failure events and sends notifications accordingly.
Implementation Snippets:
TransactionService:
Initiate transaction and emit an event:

@PostMapping("/initiate")
public Transaction initiateTransaction(@RequestBody Transaction transaction) {
    transaction.setStatus(Status.PENDING);
    transactionRepository.save(transaction);
    eventPublisher.publishStartTransactionEvent(new StartTransactionEvent(transaction.getId()));
    return transaction;
}
AccountService:
Handle transaction events, update account balance, and emit events:

@EventListener
public void handleStartTransactionEvent(StartTransactionEvent event) {
    try {
        Transaction transaction = fetchTransactionDetails(event.getTransactionId());
        processTransaction(transaction);
        eventPublisher.publishTransactionSuccessEvent(new TransactionSuccessEvent(transaction.getId()));
    } catch (AccountException e) {
        eventPublisher.publishTransactionFailureEvent(new TransactionFailureEvent(event.getTransactionId(), e.getMessage()));
    }
}
NotificationService:
Listen for transaction success/failure events and send notifications:

@EventListener
public void handleTransactionSuccessEvent(TransactionSuccessEvent event) {
    Notification notification = createSuccessNotification(event.getTransactionId());
    sendNotification(notification);
}

@EventListener
public void handleTransactionFailureEvent(TransactionFailureEvent event) {
    Notification notification = createFailureNotification(event.getTransactionId(), event.getFailureReason());
    sendNotification(notification);
}
